#version 450
// R12: a node of the render tree, filled with voxels by the card instead of by a worker thread.
//
// # What this replaces, and why a hundredfold has to come from here
//
// The ladder that fills the world in while somebody is playing asks `forge::sample` for one node at
// a time, and a node is eight voxels a side at every level. Measured: **~65,536 voxels in ~500 ms,
// 7.6 µs a voxel** (D674), and that figure is field evaluation — a walk of a several-thousand-node
// expression tree, per voxel, against hundreds of paint rules, on about half the machine's threads.
// Every scheduling fault around it has already been found and fixed: D622 took three waits out and
// was worth 4.8x, D619 took a starvation out and was worth another 3x, and what is left is not
// waiting. **D674 and D675 both concluded, from opposite ends, that a hundredfold is R12 and
// nothing short of it.** This is R12.
//
// # It brute-forces every cell, and that is not a regression
//
// `forge::sample` descends: it settles whole boxes from one evaluation at their centre plus a
// Lipschitz slack, and only walks single voxels near a surface. That descent is an OPTIMISATION of
// a per-voxel definition, and the per-voxel definition is what this shader computes. So the card
// does more evaluations than the CPU did and finishes far sooner, because 512 cells of a node are
// 512 lanes rather than 512 iterations — and because the cull the descent uses to skip a union's
// children is still here, per node, in `ws_box_away_sq`.
//
// It also means the two can be compared cell for cell, which is the whole gate: `--gpu-sample-check`
// samples the nodes the ladder itself picked a second time on the CPU and counts what differs.
// D200k, and R1a's standard before it: *"one field evaluator, mirrored, or two renderers compute
// the world two ways."*
//
// # WHY THIS IS A LITTLE MACHINE AND NOT STRAIGHT-LINE CODE
//
// **There is exactly one call to `field_eval` in this file, and it must stay that way.**
//
// The first version of this shader was written the obvious way: a `thin_feature_here` helper with
// eight calls in it, a `normal_at` with six, and three more in `main`. Seventeen. GLSL has no
// out-of-line call — the build compiles with `-O`, and spirv-opt inlines — so that was seventeen
// private copies of `field_eval`, and `field_eval` owns a 96-frame stack. A frame is fifteen words,
// so one stack is 5.8 KB per invocation and seventeen are ninety-eight, which is not a slow shader
// but a shader whose every lane is thrashing scratch memory. It also compiled to **1.05 MB of
// SPIR-V in 5.8 seconds of glslc**, which is the same fact wearing a number anybody can see.
//
// So the per-cell work is written as a loop that asks ONE question per turn and then decides what
// the answer meant. It is longer to read than the straight-line form and it is the same arithmetic
// in the same order; what it is not is seventeen stacks. If a phase is ever added here, add it to
// the machine rather than reaching for a helper with a call in it — a helper is a copy.
//
// # The per-voxel definition, exactly as `descend` reaches it at a single cell
//
// Three things about that path are easy to get subtly wrong and all three are copied deliberately:
//
//   **A single cell has a radius of NOUGHT, not half a voxel.** `box_centre` measures over the
//   sample points rather than over the cube, and says so in its own header. So every `radius` term
//   in the descent's tests — the rule-box cull, the band's span — is zero here, which is what makes
//   the settle tests collapse into the plain comparisons below.
//
//   **The bounds shape gets no thin-feature rescue at a single cell.** The rescue appears in the
//   partly-covered *box* branch and not in the single-cell one. A cell whose bounds centre is
//   outside is simply not part of the clip.
//
//   **A rule the descent could settle bypasses its own facing test.** See `GpuPaintRule::slack`.
//
// # What is NOT here, and where it still happens
//
// `apply_variation` and `forge::despeckle` are judgements over a finished clip rather than over a
// voxel — the first draws per-voxel colour variation from a hash of the cell, the second decides
// what is a deliberate stipple from a material's share of the whole clip's surface — so they stay
// on the host, over the block this hands back. R11b measured why the second cannot move: 29 of 297
// nodes come out different when the pass runs on the node rather than on the box around it.
#extension GL_GOOGLE_include_directive : require

layout(local_size_x = 64) in;

#include "field_types.glsl"
#include "field_leaf.glsl"
#include "field_walk.glsl"

// Eight voxels a side, at every level. `forge::kNodeVoxels`, and the reason the dispatch is a flat
// index with no prefix sum over the batch.
#define WS_NODE_CELLS 512u

// The phases of the machine, in the order a cell walks them.
#define PH_BOUNDS 0u   // is this cell part of the clip at all
#define PH_SOLID  1u   // the shape, at the cell's centre
#define PH_GRAD   2u   // six: which way is out, for the thin-feature rescue
#define PH_WITHIN 3u   // one: is there anything solid that way
#define PH_BEYOND 4u   // one: or is the whole feature thinner than the grid
#define PH_RULE   5u   // one per paint rule that survives its own box
#define PH_NORMAL 6u   // six: the surface normal, only where a rule asks which way it faces
#define PH_DONE   7u

// The clip mask, or the instrument's count when it has been asked for. A run under `--gpu-visits`
// builds a world whose mask is nonsense and is not trying to build one: it is measuring how many
// nodes a cell walks, which is the half of the cost no clock can separate out.
uint ws_mask_or_visits(uint mask) {
    return ((field_push.flags & WS_FIELD_FLAG_COUNT_VISITS) != 0u) ? ws_field_visits : mask;
}

// One of the six central-difference offsets, as `sub` counts 0..5: axis `sub >> 1`, plus first.
vec3 ws_offset(uint sub, float h) {
    return ws_with_axis(vec3(0.0), sub >> 1u, ((sub & 1u) == 0u) ? h : -h);
}

// How far a point is from a rule's box, squared. Nought inside it, and nought for the infinite box,
// which is what a rule whose place could not be bounded carries.
float rule_box_away_sq(uint rule, vec3 p) {
    const float lx = paint_rules.items[rule].lo[0];
    if (lx <= -1.0e29 || paint_rules.items[rule].hi[0] >= 1.0e29) return 0.0;
    const vec3 lo = vec3(lx, paint_rules.items[rule].lo[1], paint_rules.items[rule].lo[2]);
    const vec3 hi = vec3(paint_rules.items[rule].hi[0], paint_rules.items[rule].hi[1],
                         paint_rules.items[rule].hi[2]);
    const vec3 d = max(max(lo - p, p - hi), vec3(0.0));
    return dot(d, d);
}

float piece_away_sq(uint piece, vec3 p) {
    const float lx = rule_pieces.items[piece].lo[0];
    if (lx <= -1.0e29 || rule_pieces.items[piece].hi[0] >= 1.0e29) return 0.0;
    const vec3 lo = vec3(lx, rule_pieces.items[piece].lo[1], rule_pieces.items[piece].lo[2]);
    const vec3 hi = vec3(rule_pieces.items[piece].hi[0], rule_pieces.items[piece].hi[1],
                         rule_pieces.items[piece].hi[2]);
    const vec3 d = max(max(lo - p, p - hi), vec3(0.0));
    return dot(d, d);
}

// Could this rule apply here at all, without asking the field?
//
// Nowhere near the only place it could apply means it does not, and no evaluation is needed to know
// it. Free, and the difference between asking six hundred rules at every voxel and asking the two
// or three that could possibly be true here — the facility measured six of its rules at 92% of the
// whole build's field evaluations before this existed on the CPU.
bool rule_could_apply(uint rule, vec3 p) {
    if (rule_box_away_sq(rule, p) > 0.0) return false;
    // Inside the rule's overall box, but a zone made of separate pieces is mostly the space between
    // them: a weathering zone that is the great steps at the ground and the cornice wash at the top
    // has a bounding box of the whole building, which rejects nothing.
    const uint from = paint_rules.items[rule].piece_from;
    const uint to = paint_rules.items[rule].piece_to;
    if (from == to) return true;
    for (uint piece = from; piece < to; ++piece) {
        if (piece_away_sq(piece, p) <= 0.0) return true;
    }
    return false;
}

void main() {
    ws_field_visits = 0u;
    const uint gid = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;
    const uint box = gid / WS_NODE_CELLS;
    if (box >= field_push.box_count) return;
    const uint cell = gid % WS_NODE_CELLS;

    const ivec3 within_node = ivec3(cell & 7u, (cell >> 3u) & 7u, (cell >> 6u) & 7u);
    const float voxel = sample_boxes.items[box].voxel;
    const ivec3 lo = ivec3(sample_boxes.items[box].lo[0], sample_boxes.items[box].lo[1],
                           sample_boxes.items[box].lo[2]);
    // Sampled at the CENTRE of each voxel rather than its corner. `SampleSettings::sample_at_centre`
    // says why: a surface through the middle of a cell either fills it or does not, and asking at
    // the corner biases every surface half a voxel the same way — a clip a voxel narrow on two
    // sides and a voxel wide on the other two.
    const vec3 p = (vec3(lo + within_node) + 0.5) * voxel;

    uint phase = (field_push.has_bounds != 0u) ? PH_BOUNDS : PH_SOLID;
    uint sub = 0u;          // which of a phase's several questions this is
    uint rule = 0u;         // which paint rule PH_RULE is on
    uint type = 0u;         // kAir until a rule says otherwise
    uint inside = 1u;
    float dc = 0.0;         // the shape at the cell's centre
    vec3 hold = vec3(0.0);  // the plus half of a central difference, waiting for its minus
    vec3 away = vec3(0.0);  // the rescue's outward direction, then the surface normal
    vec3 reach_from = vec3(0.0);
    bool have_normal = false;
    float rule_value = 0.0;

    // A guard rather than `while (true)`: the phases are finite and so is the rule list, and a card
    // is the wrong place to discover a loop that does not end. 2 + 8 + rules + 6 is the real bound.
    const uint kGuard = 16u + 2u * field_push.rule_count;
    for (uint turn = 0u; turn < kGuard && phase != PH_DONE; ++turn) {
        // ---- which question ------------------------------------------------------------------
        uint ask_node = field_push.root;
        vec3 ask_at = p;
        if (phase == PH_BOUNDS) {
            ask_node = field_push.bounds;
        } else if (phase == PH_GRAD) {
            ask_at = p + ws_offset(sub, voxel * 0.25);
        } else if (phase == PH_WITHIN) {
            ask_at = reach_from;
        } else if (phase == PH_BEYOND) {
            ask_at = reach_from - away * voxel;
        } else if (phase == PH_NORMAL) {
            ask_at = p + ws_offset(sub, voxel);
        } else if (phase == PH_RULE) {
            // Skip whatever the boxes already answer for, without asking the field. A rule this
            // walks past is a rule the CPU's descent settles to "no" for nothing.
            while (rule < field_push.rule_count && !rule_could_apply(rule, p)) ++rule;
            if (rule >= field_push.rule_count) { phase = PH_DONE; continue; }
            ask_node = paint_rules.items[rule].test;
        }

        // ---- THE ONE CALL. See the header before adding a second ------------------------------
        const float v = field_eval(ask_node, ask_at);

        // ---- what it meant --------------------------------------------------------------------
        if (phase == PH_BOUNDS) {
            // A cell outside the bounds shape is none of the clip's business and its mask stays
            // clear, which is a different thing from a cell inside the clip and empty — that one
            // says "stamping should clear whatever is there".
            if (v > 0.0) {
                out_types.items[gid] = 0u;
                out_inside.items[gid] = ws_mask_or_visits(0u);
                return;
            }
            phase = PH_SOLID;
        } else if (phase == PH_SOLID) {
            dc = v;
            if (dc <= 0.0) {
                phase = PH_RULE;                       // solid; go and paint it
            } else if (dc < voxel * field_push.half_cell &&
                       (field_push.flags & WS_FIELD_FLAG_NO_RESCUE) == 0u) {
                phase = PH_GRAD;                       // outside, but near enough to be a thin feature
                sub = 0u;
            } else {
                out_types.items[gid] = 0u;             // outside, and nothing here to lose
                out_inside.items[gid] = ws_mask_or_visits(inside);
                return;
            }
        } else if (phase == PH_GRAD) {
            // Which way is out. An SDF's gradient points away from the surface, and central
            // differences over a quarter of a voxel are steady enough for a direction even where
            // the field is not smooth.
            const uint axis = sub >> 1u;
            if ((sub & 1u) == 0u) {
                hold = ws_with_axis(hold, axis, v);
            } else {
                away = ws_with_axis(away, axis, ws_axis_of(hold, axis) - v);
            }
            ++sub;
            if (sub >= 6u) {
                const float len = length(away);
                if (len < 1.0e-12) {
                    out_types.items[gid] = 0u;         // no direction; there is nothing to rescue
                    out_inside.items[gid] = ws_mask_or_visits(inside);
                    return;
                }
                away /= len;
                // Just inside the surface. `dc` is how far out this cell's centre is.
                reach_from = p - away * (dc + voxel * 0.05);
                phase = PH_WITHIN;
            }
        } else if (phase == PH_WITHIN) {
            // Nothing solid that way means the field was near zero here for some other reason and
            // there is no feature to rescue.
            if (v > 0.0) {
                out_types.items[gid] = 0u;
                out_inside.items[gid] = ws_mask_or_visits(inside);
                return;
            }
            phase = PH_BEYOND;
        } else if (phase == PH_BEYOND) {
            // And a whole voxel further in. Still solid means a wall, and a wall does not need
            // rescuing — this is what keeps the flat faces of the building exactly where they were.
            // Out the far side means the whole feature is thinner than the grid can hold, and the
            // cell is kept: a mullion half a voxel wide covers no centre, so without this it is not
            // thinned but deleted outright.
            if (v <= 0.0) {
                out_types.items[gid] = 0u;
                out_inside.items[gid] = ws_mask_or_visits(inside);
                return;
            }
            phase = PH_RULE;
            away = vec3(0.0);
            hold = vec3(0.0);
        } else if (phase == PH_RULE) {
            rule_value = v;
            const float low = paint_rules.items[rule].low;
            const float high = paint_rules.items[rule].high;
            if (rule_value < low || rule_value > high) {
                ++rule;
            } else if (paint_rules.items[rule].slack >= 1.0e30 &&
                       paint_rules.items[rule].facing_axis < 3u && !have_normal) {
                // Only a rule the descent could settle for NOTHING reaches the facing test — see
                // GpuPaintRule::slack. Six more evaluations, and only ever taken once a cell.
                phase = PH_NORMAL;
                sub = 0u;
            } else {
                if (paint_rules.items[rule].slack >= 1.0e30 &&
                    paint_rules.items[rule].facing_axis < 3u) {
                    const float component = ws_axis_of(away, paint_rules.items[rule].facing_axis);
                    const float want = paint_rules.items[rule].facing_min;
                    const bool passes = (want >= 0.0) ? (component >= want) : (component <= want);
                    if (!passes) { ++rule; continue; }
                }
                type = paint_rules.items[rule].type;
                ++rule;
            }
            if (phase == PH_RULE && rule >= field_push.rule_count) phase = PH_DONE;
        } else if (phase == PH_NORMAL) {
            const uint axis = sub >> 1u;
            if ((sub & 1u) == 0u) {
                hold = ws_with_axis(hold, axis, v);
            } else {
                away = ws_with_axis(away, axis, ws_axis_of(hold, axis) - v);
            }
            ++sub;
            if (sub >= 6u) {
                // `forge::normalise`'s own answer for a zero vector, which is {0,1,0} rather than
                // {0,0,0}: a rule keyed on "up" then matches, which is what the CPU does.
                const float len = length(away);
                away = (len > 0.0) ? (away / len) : vec3(0.0, 1.0, 0.0);
                have_normal = true;
                phase = PH_RULE;   // and back to the rule that asked, with `rule` unchanged
            }
        }
    }

    // A cell with matter in it and no rule that matched is still matter. It would be worse to drop
    // it silently than to give it the first type asked for, because a hole in a wall is harder to
    // notice than a wrong colour.
    if (type == 0u && field_push.rule_count > 0u) type = field_push.first_type;

    out_types.items[gid] = type;
    out_inside.items[gid] = ws_mask_or_visits(inside);
}
